[submodule "docs/site"]
	path = docs/site
	url = https://github.com/Night-Traders-Dev/SageVM-Docs

## .deps/SageLang used to be a submodule here, and it closed a cycle:
##
##   SageLang/core/src/sage/vm-tools  ->  SageVM/.deps/SageLang  ->  SageLang
##
## That is not theoretical. `git clone --recurse-submodules` on SageLang produced
## three levels of vm-tools/.deps/SageLang and 1.6 GB for what is fundamentally two
## repositories, and git 2.53 reported no cycle at all. The `fetchRecurseSubmodules`
## = false that stood guard on both sides does not help: that setting applies to
## fetch and `submodule update`, not to an explicit `clone --recurse-submodules`,
## so it was inert exactly where it was needed. I verified this by cloning rather
## than by reading the manual.
##
## The submodule is gone. sagemake now resolves SageLang instead of vendoring it,
## in this order:
##
##   1. $SAGELANG_DIR          -- explicit override
##   2. ../SageLang            -- sibling checkout, the normal case
##   3. .deps/SageLang         -- a hand-placed checkout still works
##   4. /usr/local             -- a system install, if `sage` is on PATH
##
## To restore a submodule-based build, clone SageLang yourself and point
## $SAGELANG_DIR at it. Do not re-add the submodule: that is what caused this.
